WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.962
From the controller's perspective, retrieval is just a function.

00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:07.497
You give it a query and perhaps a top K value.

00:00:07.497 --> 00:00:17.357
It returns evidence. Behind that function, you might use Chroma, P G vector, Elasticsearch, or a hosted vector service.

00:00:17.357 --> 00:00:19.309
The controller should not care.

00:00:19.782 --> 00:00:22.109
This is an example of abstraction.

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:26.354
The storage technology is hidden behind a stable contract.

00:00:26.354 --> 00:00:29.989
Suppose your first project uses Chroma locally.

00:00:29.989 --> 00:00:35.509
Later, a company deploys the same workflow with a managed vector database.

00:00:35.509 --> 00:00:41.789
If the retrieval function still accepts the same inputs and returns the same evidence structure,

00:00:41.789 --> 00:00:43.894
the graph does not need to change.

00:00:44.392 --> 00:00:49.732
Design the interface first. Keep infrastructure details inside the tool.
